```java
public class BankAccount {
    private double balance;        // only this class can access
    String accountType;            // default — same package only
    protected String ownerId;      // same package + subclasses
    public String bankName;        // everyone

    public double getBalance() {   // public getter for private field
        return balance;
    }

    private void validateTransaction() {  // private helper method
        // only used internally
    }
}
```

**Best practice**: Make fields `private`, expose only what's necessary via `public` methods.

```java
public class Person {
    private String name;   // hidden — can't access directly from outside
    private int age;

    public String getName() {     // getter — controlled read access
        return name;
    }

    public void setAge(int age) { // setter — with validation
        if (age < 0 || age > 150) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid age: " + age);
        }
        this.age = age;
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
}

// External code can't corrupt the object's state:
Person p = new Person();
// p.age = -5;  // compile error — private
p.setAge(-5);   // throws IllegalArgumentException — validation!
```

```java
// In package com.company.base:
public class Vehicle {
    int speed;           // default — same package only
    protected int fuel;  // protected — same package + subclasses anywhere
}

// In package com.company.vehicles: (DIFFERENT package)
public class Car extends Vehicle {
    void drive() {
        // fuel = 100;   // OK — protected, Car is a subclass
        // speed = 60;   // COMPILE ERROR — default, different package
    }
}
```

`protected` is designed for the template method pattern and inheritance hierarchies — parent class exposes implementation hooks to subclasses without making them fully public.

```java
public class Temperature {
    private double celsius;  // PRIVATE — implementation detail

    public void setCelsius(double celsius) {  // PUBLIC — part of API
        if (celsius < -273.15) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Below absolute zero");
        this.celsius = celsius;
    }

    public double getCelsius() { return celsius; }          // PUBLIC
    public double getFahrenheit() { return celsius * 9/5 + 32; }  // PUBLIC

    // Tomorrow I could change storage to Kelvin internally
    // and none of the callers would break — they only call public methods
}
```

The private field `celsius` is an implementation detail. If you later change to storing temperature in Kelvin internally, no calling code breaks — they only depend on the public methods.
